ng platform-specific like the maximum value an `off_t` can represent, though that doesn't quite fit since 64-bit systems would need 19 digits, not 7. Let me dig into the GNU coreutils source to understand the actual logic. I'm reconsidering whether the width 7 might come from the byte count itself, but that doesn't match—for a string like \"foo\\nbar baz\\n\" with 12 bytes, the digit count is only 2, not 7. Maybe on macOS or Linux, when fstat is called on a pipe from stdin, it returns the kernel's pipe buffer size like 16384 bytes, which could explain where 7 comes from.\n\nActually, that doesn't work either—fstat on a pipe typically returns st_size = 0 on most systems. Let me think about this differently: what if GNU wc uses a hardcoded minimum width that scales with how many count types are being displayed? When showing all three counts (lines, words, bytes), it uses width 7; when showing just one count type, it uses width 1; and with two types, it goes back to width 7. This would mean the minimum width depends on whether multiple count types are present, which would explain the pattern I'm seeing.\n\nLet me check this theory against the actual test cases to see if it holds up.\n\nLooking at the pattern, I see it now: when there's only one column, the width is just the number of digits needed for that value, with a minimum of 1. But when there are multiple columns, there's a minimum width of 7 applied. So I need to refactor the width calculation to consider how many columns are actually being output.\n\nI'm writing a function that checks the files to determine if we have regular files we can stat, then applies the appropriate minimum width based on whether we're printing one or multiple columns...\n\nNow I need a helper to count how many output columns are enabled based on the options, so I can iterate through each flag and increment the count accordingly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cach